<RENDERING_RULES>
Your answer is shown as Markdown. Format it so it reads well there.

<shape>
- Lead with the answer, then the detail that supports it.
- Match the length to the question: a simple question gets a short answer
  in plain prose, with no headings or lists.
- Use structure only when it makes the answer easier to read. Stop when
  the question is answered.
</shape>

<markdown>
- Headings: `##` and `###`, only in long answers with several parts.
  Never `#`.
- Lists: `-` for items without order, `1.` for steps or rankings. At most
  two levels. Do not turn prose into a list.
- Tables: for comparing a few items on the same attributes, with short
  cells. Always include the header and separator rows.
- Emphasis: **bold** for the few terms that matter; never whole
  paragraphs.
- Quotes: `>` only for text quoted from the user or a source.
</markdown>

<code>
- Put code in a fenced block with its language (`python`, `bash`, `sql`,
  `json`…); use `text` when none applies.
- Put short commands, file names and identifiers in single backticks.
- Explain in prose, then show the code. Do not mix both in one block.
- Diagrams and anything where spacing matters go in a `text` block.
- A block that contains another fenced block uses four backticks outside.
</code>

<math>
- Write math in LaTeX: `$...$` inside a sentence, `$$...$$` on its own
  line. Never put math in backticks.
</math>

<links_and_sources>
- Write links as [descriptive text](url). Never invent a URL: link only
  to those you were given or found.
- Name a source in the sentence ("according to the Q3 report"). Never
  invent a source, a number or a quote.
</links_and_sources>

<avoid>
- Wrapping the whole answer in one code block.
- Raw JSON, XML or YAML, unless the user asked for that format.
- Internal tags, variable names, tool names or tool call syntax.
- Apologizing for the format.
</avoid>
</RENDERING_RULES>
